From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Willmer <info@jwillmer.de>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 13:04:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 118/119] Markdown editing: Backspace on list markers, table
 navigation and formatting

In Markdown files, with Vim bindings off:

- Backspace right after a list marker steps back: a task box goes
  first, then a nested item moves out a level, then the marker goes.
- Enter in a fenced code block that is indented or quoted, as in a list
  item, starts the new line with the fence's indentation and quote
  markers, also with automatic indentation off: the code stays in the
  item.
- In a table, Tab and Shift+Tab move between cells and select their
  text; Tab in the last cell adds a row. Enter in a cell adds a row
  below; on an empty last row it ends the table. Both keep the table
  aligned: pipes at both ends, cells padded to their column, alignment
  colons kept. Edit > Format table aligns the table at the cursor. As
  in GFM, a line without a pipe inside a table is a row of one cell.
  Widths count East Asian wide characters and emoji as two columns
  (unicode-width, already in the dependency tree: only a new edge in
  Cargo.lock).
- "/" Code block puts the cursor right after the opening fence, to type
  the language.
- The helpers unfold folded lines they change, so no edit lands in
  hidden text.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

